===============================================================================
PART 1 : WHAT IT IS, AND WHY IT EXISTS
===============================================================================

DEFINITION

    Abstraction = showing WHAT an object does, and hiding HOW it does it.

    You expose the method SIGNATURE and hide the method BODY.

    Car analogy: you press the brake pedal. That is the WHAT. Whether the
    car uses disc brakes, drum brakes or regenerative braking is the HOW,
    and you neither know nor care. If the manufacturer swaps the HOW next
    year, your driving does not change.


WHY IT EXISTS

    Because some classes exist only to define a CONTRACT, and have no
    sensible behaviour of their own.

    Think about "Shape". Every shape has an area. But what is the area of
    a plain Shape? There is no answer. A Shape that is not a circle, not a
    square, not a rectangle is not a thing that exists.

    Java lets you say exactly that:

        abstract class Shape {
            abstract double area();      // every shape HAS one...
        }                                // ...but I refuse to say what it is

    Two things now become true, enforced by the compiler:

        1. new Shape()  is a COMPILE ERROR. The meaningless object cannot
           be created at all.

        2. Any class that extends Shape is FORCED to write area(), or it
           will not compile.

    That second point is the real power. An abstract method is a promise
    that the compiler collects for you.


THE ONE-LINE REASON

    Abstraction lets you program against a CONTRACT instead of against a
    CLASS -- so you can change the implementation without touching the
    calling code.


===============================================================================
PART 2 : THE TWO TOOLS
===============================================================================

Java gives you two ways to achieve abstraction. You need both.

    ABSTRACT CLASS      partial abstraction
                        some methods have bodies, some do not
                        "IS-A, and here is some shared code to help you"

    INTERFACE           (was) full abstraction
                        a pure contract -- names only, no shared state
                        "CAN-DO -- whoever signs this must provide these"

Quick shape of each:

    abstract class Animal {              interface Flyable {
        String name;                         void fly();
        Animal(String name) {            }
            this.name = name;
        }
        void sleep() {                   class Bird extends Animal
            System.out.println("zzz");         implements Flyable {
        }                                    public void fly() { ... }
        abstract void sound();               void sound() { ... }
    }                                    }

Note that last class: Bird EXTENDS one abstract class and IMPLEMENTS an
interface at the same time. That is the normal, professional shape of real
Java code, and it is where this topic is heading.

You cannot extend two classes. You can implement as many interfaces as you
like. That single fact drives most of the "which should I use" decisions.


===============================================================================
PART 3 : YOUR OWN CODE -- THE BUG ABSTRACTION FIXES
===============================================================================

Open OOPS/Polymorphism/ShapesExample.java. It currently starts like this:

        public class ShapesExample {
            void area(){
                System.out.println("This is the shape class example");
            }
        }

and main() contains:

        ShapesExample shape = new ShapesExample();
        shape.area();          // prints "This is the shape class example"

Look hard at those two lines. You created a Shape that is not any shape,
and asked it for its area, and it answered with a sentence that is not an
area. Nothing is wrong syntactically. Everything is wrong in meaning.

That placeholder body exists for exactly one reason: Java forced you to
write SOMETHING so the children had a method to override. You did not want
that body. You were working around the language.

`abstract` is the feature that removes the workaround:

        abstract class ShapesExample {
            abstract void area();        // no body. None is needed.
        }

Now:

        new ShapesExample();     -> COMPILE ERROR, and that is correct
        class Cone extends ShapesExample { }
                                 -> COMPILE ERROR, Cone forgot area()

The nonsense object became impossible, and forgetting to override became
impossible. You will rewrite this file properly in AbstractClass/ShapeArea.java.

Same story in OOPS/Inheritance -- LivingThingAnimalDog, VehicleCarElectricCar,
ProductElectronicLaptop. In every one of them the top class is a concept,
not a thing. Every one of them should be abstract.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : WHERE ABSTRACTION SITS AMONG THE FOUR PILLARS
===============================================================================

        1. Encapsulation -- hide the DATA                    [done]
        2. Inheritance   -- reuse behaviour through IS-A     [done]
        3. Polymorphism  -- one name, many forms             [done]
        4. Abstraction   -- hide the IMPLEMENTATION          [you are here]

Abstraction is not a fourth independent idea. It is what the other three
were building towards:

        Inheritance   gives you the IS-A link.
        Polymorphism  lets a parent reference run a child's method.
        Abstraction   removes the parent's method body, so the child MUST
                      supply one and the parent can never be instantiated.

Read your own Polymorphism/Notes, PART 6. It ends with:

        "abstraction is what makes polymorphism genuinely useful in real
         code. So after this topic, go straight to abstract classes and
         interfaces -- that is where these two ideas join up."

This is that file. The three conditions for runtime polymorphism you
memorised -- inheritance, overriding, upcasting -- are all still required.
Abstraction just makes conditions 1 and 2 compulsory instead of optional.


===============================================================================
PART 5 : ABSTRACTION vs ENCAPSULATION
===============================================================================

You already have this table in Encapsulation/Notes PART 10. It is repeated
here from the other side, because this is asked in almost every interview.

                        ABSTRACTION                 ENCAPSULATION
    ------------------  --------------------------  ------------------------
    Hides               IMPLEMENTATION (the how)    DATA (the state)
    Question answered   "what does this do?"        "who may touch this?"
    Achieved using      abstract class, interface   private + getters/setters
    Level               Design level                Implementation level
    Purpose             Hide the complexity         Protect the data
    Focus               The OUTSIDE view of a class The INSIDE of a class
    Example             interface Payment           private double balance

ONE SENTENCE

    Abstraction hides the COMPLEXITY.
    Encapsulation hides the DATA.

They are not rivals. Encapsulation is usually HOW you implement abstraction:
you hide the fields (encapsulation) behind methods that describe intent
(abstraction).

A trap to watch for: people say "abstraction hides implementation, so a
private method is abstraction". No. A private method is encapsulation.
Abstraction is about the TYPE you program against, not about visibility.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : HOW TO DECIDE -- ABSTRACT CLASS OR INTERFACE?
===============================================================================

The full comparison table lives in Interfaces/Notes PART 8. Here is the
decision itself, which is what you actually need at the keyboard.

ASK THESE IN ORDER

    1. Is the relationship IS-A or CAN-DO?

           Dog IS-A Animal            -> abstract class Animal
           Dog CAN-DO Swimmable       -> interface Swimmable

       IS-A describes what the thing fundamentally is. CAN-DO describes an
       ability that could belong to unrelated things. A Duck, a Submarine
       and a Fish are all Swimmable and share no ancestor at all.

    2. Do the implementations need to SHARE STATE or SHARED CODE?

           yes -> abstract class (it can have fields and a constructor)
           no  -> interface (it cannot have instance fields at all)

    3. Do the implementations already have a parent, or might they need
       an unrelated one later?

           yes -> interface. A class gets only ONE superclass, ever, and
                  spending it is a permanent decision.

    4. Is this a capability you want to bolt onto many unrelated types?

           yes -> interface. Comparable, Runnable and Serializable are all
                  exactly this.

RULE OF THUMB

    Default to an INTERFACE. Reach for an ABSTRACT CLASS only when you have
    real shared state or shared code to give away.

    This is the modern advice and it is the opposite of what older tutorials
    say, because before Java 8 an interface could not hold any code at all.

THEY ARE NOT EXCLUSIVE -- the professional pattern uses both together:

        interface  Payable              <- the contract, for anyone
        abstract class Employee         <- shared fields + shared code
              implements Payable
        class Manager extends Employee  <- the concrete thing
        class Intern  extends Employee

    You will build exactly this in AbstractClass/EmployeePayroll.java.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : THE VOCABULARY YOU WILL HEAR
===============================================================================

    ABSTRACT METHOD       a method with a signature and no body, ending in
                          a semicolon:   abstract void draw();

    CONCRETE METHOD       an ordinary method, with a body

    CONCRETE CLASS        an ordinary class -- one you CAN instantiate

    CONTRACT              the set of methods a type promises to provide.
                          An interface IS a contract.

    IMPLEMENTATION        the code that fulfils the contract

    PROGRAM TO AN         declare variables using the general type:
    INTERFACE                 List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
                          not      ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
                          The single most repeated piece of Java advice.

    LOOSE COUPLING        the result of the above -- calling code depends on
                          the contract, so the implementation can be swapped

    PARTIAL / FULL        abstract class = partial abstraction
    ABSTRACTION           interface = full abstraction  (see the trap below)

    TRAP: "an interface gives 100% abstraction" was true until Java 8. Since
    default methods exist, an interface can contain real code, so it is no
    longer 100% abstract. Say "it was designed as a pure contract, and since
    Java 8 it may also carry default methods". That answer shows you know
    which Java you are writing. You are on Java 21.


===============================================================================
PART 8 : WHY THIS TOPIC UNLOCKS COLLECTIONS
===============================================================================

This is the practical reason to do abstraction before Collections rather
than after.

The Collections Framework is a set of INTERFACES with several
implementations each:

        Collection            (interface)
            List              (interface)     ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector
            Set               (interface)     HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet
            Queue             (interface)     ArrayDeque, PriorityQueue
        Map                   (interface)     HashMap, LinkedHashMap, TreeMap

So the standard line everyone writes:

        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

is a parent-type reference holding a child object -- upcasting, from
Polymorphism/Notes PART 4 -- where the parent type is an INTERFACE. If you
had not done abstraction, that line would be unreadable.

And it is written that way on purpose. Swapping to

        List<String> names = new LinkedList<>();

changes the performance characteristics and breaks NOTHING, because every
other line in the program only ever used the List contract. That is PART 7's
"program to an interface", and it is the whole reason Collections is shaped
this way.

You will also meet these interfaces immediately:

        Comparable<T>     one method, compareTo()  -- gives a class its
                          natural sort order, used by Collections.sort()
        Comparator<T>     one method, compare()    -- an alternative sort
                          order, supplied from outside
        Iterable<T>       one method, iterator()   -- this is what makes
                          the for-each loop work on any collection

Each of those is a functional interface (Interfaces/Notes PART 7), which is
also the doorway to lambdas and streams later.


