PART 9 : READING ORDER AND FILES IN THIS PACKAGE
===============================================================================

    Notes                              <- you are reading this (overview)
    IntroToAbstraction.java            <- both tools side by side, runnable

    AbstractClass/Notes                <- rules, constructors, traps
    AbstractClass/AbstractClassDemo.java   <- worked example, runnable
    AbstractClass/ShapeArea.java           <- PRACTICE 1 (fix ShapesExample)
    AbstractClass/GameCharacter.java       <- PRACTICE 2 (shared state)

    Interfaces/Notes                   <- rules, default/static, diamond
    Interfaces/InterfaceDemo.java          <- worked example, runnable
    Interfaces/MultiFunctionMachine.java   <- PRACTICE 3 (many interfaces)
    Interfaces/HotelBookingApp.java        <- PRACTICE 4 (both together)

Do them in that order. Practice 4 is the capstone -- it uses an abstract
class and an interface in the same program, which is what real code looks
like.


===============================================================================
PART 10 : TEST YOURSELF BEFORE YOU START CODING
===============================================================================

Come back to these AFTER the two sub-Notes. Answer out loud, no looking.

    1. What is the difference between abstraction and encapsulation?
    2. Can you create an object of an abstract class? What about an
       interface?
    3. Can an abstract class have a constructor? If it can never be
       instantiated, who calls it and when?
    4. Can an abstract class have zero abstract methods? Is that useful?
    5. Can an abstract method be private? static? final? Why not, in each
       case?
    6. Why can a class implement many interfaces but extend only one class?
    7. Two interfaces define the same default method and one class
       implements both. What happens, and how do you fix it?
    8. What is a functional interface, and name three from the JDK.
    9. Why is  List<String> x = new ArrayList<>();  preferred over
       ArrayList<String> x = new ArrayList<>();
   10. IS-A or CAN-DO: Dog/Animal, Dog/Swimmable, Circle/Shape,
       Circle/Drawable, Manager/Employee, Manager/Payable.
